providerResponses.text:
{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "You can explain Bayes' rule in your own words, correctly compute the posterior probability for a changed medical-test example while keeping the base rate distinct from sensitivity, and state the method you used and why."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "MASTERY",
          "outcome": "Develop the ability to explain Bayes’ rule and apply it to a changed medical-test probability example. I confuse the base rate with sensitivity.",
          "explanation": "The reported weakness is a specific confusion between the base rate (prevalence, P(D)) and sensitivity (P(+|D)). The smallest useful strategy is one integrated activity that (a) explains the rule while explicitly separating these two quantities, (b) works a changed medical-test example end-to-end with the actual arithmetic shown and checked, and (c) asks for a brief self-explanation of the method choice. A separate contingent activity is reserved only for the case where the learner's own worked example still shows the base-rate/sensitivity swap. No extra practice sets, no second check of already verified items, and no phase ceremony are warranted.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 45,
            "likelyMinutes": 70,
            "highMinutes": 110
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_core",
              "title": "Explain and apply Bayes' rule to a changed medical-test example",
              "outcome": "A correct, explained posterior probability for a changed medical-test example, with base rate and sensitivity kept distinct.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 45,
                "likelyMinutes": 70,
                "highMinutes": 110
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "The learner computes the posterior correctly, identifies which number is the base rate and which is the sensitivity, and explains the rule without conflating the two."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The core correction: define prevalence/base rate P(D) separately from sensitivity P(+|D), and use both correctly in the worked example."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The rule itself: state and explain Bayes' rule, then apply it step by step to the changed medical-test example."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Transfer: the worked example uses changed numbers and a changed test so the learner must apply the rule rather than recall a memorized answer."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "bayes_integrated",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_core"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3"
            ],
            "title": "Explain Bayes' rule and solve a changed medical-test example",
            "activityType": "Integrated explanation and application",
            "purpose": "Separate base rate from sensitivity in explanation, then apply Bayes' rule correctly to a changed medical-test example with the arithmetic shown and checked.",
            "instructions": "Work through this in one sitting, writing as you go. Use only the standard Bayes' rule and conditional-probability methods already taught to you; do not assume any advanced methods beyond that repertoire.\n\n1. Write your own one-paragraph explanation of Bayes' rule. In it, explicitly define: the base rate (prevalence) as P(D), the sensitivity as P(+|D), the false-positive rate as P(+|not D), and the posterior P(D|+). State in one sentence how the base rate and sensitivity differ and why swapping them is wrong.\n\n2. Use this changed medical-test example: a disease has prevalence P(D) = 0.02. A test has sensitivity P(+|D) = 0.90 and false-positive rate P(+|not D) = 0.05. A person tests positive. Compute P(D|+).\n\n3. Show every step: write the law of total probability for P(+), substitute the numbers, compute the numerator P(+|D)P(D), compute the denominator, and divide. Keep the base rate and sensitivity in separate labeled lines so the two cannot be confused. Give the final posterior as a decimal and a percentage.\n\n4. Check your answer by a second route: compute P(not D|+) from the same denominator and confirm the two posteriors sum to 1. If they do not, find and correct the actual error before finishing.\n\n5. Write two or three sentences on why you used Bayes' rule here and what would change in the answer if the base rate were much higher while sensitivity stayed the same. Do not claim a general rule that arbitrary parameter changes would not guarantee; state what your specific numbers show.",
            "completionCriterion": "A written explanation that defines base rate and sensitivity separately, a fully shown computation of P(D|+) for the changed example that is numerically correct, a cross-check that P(D|+) + P(not D|+) = 1, and a short correct statement of the method used and the effect of changing the base rate in this example.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 45,
              "likelyMinutes": 70,
              "highMinutes": 110
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 90,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 40,
                "completionCriterion": "Explanation of Bayes' rule written with base rate and sensitivity defined separately, and the numerator and denominator of the changed example set up with numbers substituted."
              }
            ],
            "cognitiveDemand": "MODERATE"
          },
          {
            "localId": "bayes_remediation",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_core"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2"
            ],
            "title": "Targeted base-rate vs sensitivity correction",
            "activityType": "Failure-triggered remediation",
            "purpose": "Repair the specific base-rate/sensitivity swap only if the integrated activity's own written work still shows it.",
            "instructions": "Re-read your written work from the integrated activity and locate the exact line where the base rate and sensitivity were swapped or conflated. Rewrite that line correctly, then redo only the affected arithmetic step of the same example and confirm the corrected posterior. Do not redo the whole assignment or add unrelated practice.",
            "completionCriterion": "The specific swapped or conflated line is corrected, the affected arithmetic is redone correctly, and the corrected posterior matches the cross-check.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 15,
              "likelyMinutes": 25,
              "highMinutes": 40
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 40,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "CONTINGENT",
              "trigger": {
                "checkpointActivityId": "bayes_integrated",
                "condition": "The learner's written work from bayes_integrated still uses the base rate where the sensitivity belongs, or the final posterior is wrong because the two were swapped."
              }
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "bayes_integrated"
            ],
            "cognitiveDemand": "LIGHT"
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "The single integrated activity covers explanation, application and transfer together rather than as separate stages, because the reported weakness is one specific confusion and a separate practice set would add no distinct evidence.",
            "The remediation activity is contingent, not committed, so the plan does not silently extend the assignment when the integrated work already succeeds."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "No prior performance data is available, so the effort estimate is a first-pass range and may be revised after the integrated activity is completed."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when the integrated activity's completion criterion is met: correct explained posterior for the changed example, with base rate and sensitivity kept distinct and the cross-check passing.",
            "If the integrated activity succeeds cleanly, do not start the contingent remediation activity."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "This plan trades breadth for precision: it commits only one integrated activity that directly attacks the reported base-rate/sensitivity confusion through explanation, a changed medical-test computation with shown arithmetic, and a cross-check, rather than adding separate relearning, practice and check stages. The targeted remediation is contingent on the learner's own written work still showing the swap, so no extra work is booked unless the evidence calls for it. The timebox is 90 minutes with one genuine split point after 40 minutes, matching the comfortable-early preference without treating the timebox as a floor or a reason to multiply sessions."
}

